pySMESH: make the EXPRESSION distribution of StdMeshers_NumberOfSegments integrate its density
to a relative tolerance, and fail loudly when it cannot (SMESH V9_16_0; applies after
StdMeshers_Distribution_table.patch).

Defect: NumberOfSegments with DT_ExprFunc places node k of N where the integral of the density
expression, from the start of the EDGE, reaches k/N of its total. The integral is wrong for any
density that one fixed Gauss rule cannot resolve, and a failed integral returns 0 with no error.

Symptom (15 m straight EDGE, N = 100, density 1/(a+t), exact node t_k = a(((1+a)/a)^(k/N)-1)):
the largest node error is 1.3 cm for a = 0.03, 0.95 m for a = 3e-3, 10.2 m for a = 3e-4 and
11.1 m for a = 3e-5, with the table patch applied and without it alike. The compute reports no
error. A density with a pole between the 501 points that CheckExpressionFunction samples, such
as 1/(t-0.3001)^2, is meshed from a finite total although its integral diverges.

Root cause: StdMeshers_Distribution.cxx:230-244 (FunctionExpr::integral) integrates with one
math_GaussSingleIntegration of order 20 over the whole interval. For the total that interval is
[0, 1]; a peaked density is underestimated, so J = F(1)/N is too small, the nodes crowd towards
the peak, and the last segment takes the rest of the EDGE. On !IsDone() or an exception it
returns 0 and logs only a MESSAGE. The code is unchanged since SMESH 6b471bcc5 (2005-12-28);
upstream master at 83f8bf482 (2026-09-14) has it unchanged.

Change, in FunctionExpr::integral only:
* math_KronrodSingleIntegration(f, a, b, 15, 1e-12, 1000): adaptive Gauss-Kronrod with the
  relative tolerance 1e-12 and at most 1000 refinements. a == b gives 0.
* !IsDone() (the density cannot be evaluated at a point of [a, b]) throws Standard_Failure
  that names the interval.
* OCCT keeps IsDone() true when its iteration limit or its stagnation test (50 refinements
  that change nothing) ends the loop, and when a NaN error ends it at once. So a reached
  relative error above 1e-12, or NaN, also throws Standard_Failure, with the interval, the
  error and the iteration count. SMESH_subMesh::ComputeStateEngine records the exception as
  the EDGE's compute error (COMPERR_OCC_EXCEPTION), so the compute fails naming the EDGE.

Not changed: FunctionExpr::value; the TABLE distribution; buildDistribution (the table patch
already solves every node against the running total); StdMeshers_CartesianParameters3D, whose
spacing functions call FunctionExpr::value only, never integral, so no Cartesian grid moves.

diff --git a/src/StdMeshers/StdMeshers_Distribution.cxx b/src/StdMeshers/StdMeshers_Distribution.cxx
--- a/src/StdMeshers/StdMeshers_Distribution.cxx
+++ b/src/StdMeshers/StdMeshers_Distribution.cxx
@@ -28,8 +28,9 @@
 //
 #include "StdMeshers_Distribution.hxx"
 
-#include <math_GaussSingleIntegration.hxx>
+#include <math_KronrodSingleIntegration.hxx>
 #include <utilities.h>
+#include "SMESH_Comment.hxx"
 
 #include <Standard_Failure.hxx>
 #include <Expr_NamedUnknown.hxx>
@@ -229,18 +230,35 @@
 
 double FunctionExpr::integral( const double a, const double b ) const
 {
-  double res = 0.0;
-  try {
-    OCC_CATCH_SIGNALS;
-    math_GaussSingleIntegration _int
-      ( *static_cast<math_Function*>( const_cast<FunctionExpr*> (this) ), a, b, 20 );
-    if( _int.IsDone() )
-      res = _int.Value();
-  } catch(Standard_Failure&) {
-    res = 0.0;
-    MESSAGE( "Exception in integral calculating" );
-  }
-  return res;
+  // pySMESH: one 20-point Gauss rule over [a,b] underestimated a peaked density by orders of
+  // magnitude, and a failure returned 0 with no error. The adaptive Gauss-Kronrod rule
+  // integrates to a relative tolerance; a result that misses it raises instead.
+  if( a == b )
+    return 0.0;
+  const double tolerance = 1e-12;
+  const int    maxNbIter = 1000;
+  math_KronrodSingleIntegration _int
+    ( *static_cast<math_Function*>( const_cast<FunctionExpr*> (this) ), a, b,
+      /*NbPnts=*/15, tolerance, maxNbIter );
+  if( !_int.IsDone() )
+  {
+    SMESH_Comment msg( "StdMeshers::FunctionExpr::integral: the density cannot be evaluated"
+                       " in [" );
+    msg << a << ", " << b << "]";
+    throw Standard_Failure( msg.c_str() );
+  }
+  // OCCT keeps IsDone() true when the iteration limit or its stagnation test stops the
+  // refinement, and a NaN error ends the loop at once; so the reached error is checked.
+  if( !( _int.ErrorReached() <= tolerance ))
+  {
+    SMESH_Comment msg( "StdMeshers::FunctionExpr::integral: the adaptive integral of the"
+                       " density over [" );
+    msg << a << ", " << b << "] did not converge (relative error " << _int.ErrorReached()
+        << " after " << _int.NbIterReached() << " iterations); the density may have a pole"
+        << " or no finite integral";
+    throw Standard_Failure( msg.c_str() );
+  }
+  return _int.Value();
 }
 
 double dihotomySolve( Function& f, const double val, const double _start, const double _fin, const double eps, bool& ok )
